INT. FLOWER WHOLESALE MARKET - 4 A.M.

Buckets of roses. Concrete floor slick with water. Cold light.

HARLAN, 50s, dock boss, counts stems. KENDRA, 30s, buyer, checks a list. TOBY, 20s, runner, hauls a cart. SOLOMON, 60s, auctioneer, rings a handbell. BIANCA, 40s, florist, argues over a price. GREER, 30s, plainclothes, stands unnoticed by the lilies.

Harlan sees the wire in the lilies. He drops the clipboard. Kendra hears the clatter and looks up. She sees Greer's badge, then Toby's cart, and she slides a hand into her bag.

Toby shoves the cart. It rolls into Greer. Roses fly. Bianca screams. Solomon rings the bell like a fire alarm.

Greer stumbles but draws. Harlan raises both hands. Kendra's hand stays in her bag. Greer points at her.

Everyone stops.
